# One robot's life: connect and hand over the credential, make sure the Player
# exists, chat on the world channel and read it back, add an item, earn
# exp (the reward mail it sends is then listed and claimed), gift an item to
# itself through the saga and claim it back, watch a gift to nobody get
# compensated, clear a dungeon run,
# queue for a duel and then a ranked match, earn enough exp to level up (which sends a reward mail
# through the effect chain), queue for a duel until matched, read the World's
# counters. Run an even number of robots: duels need pairs. Param keys are the request fields' snake_case
# names; a value can also come from the robot's blackboard.
#
# The same file is the demo's regression test: `go run ./cmd/loadtest` fails
# when any step returns a non-zero code or the thresholds are exceeded.
scenarios:
  - name: demo
    node:
      sequence:
        - action: connect
        - action: enter_game
        # The scene: from here the server replicates this client's own state
        # and every other online player's, as versioned deltas. Installed
        # right after the login that joined it — a capture installed later
        # would miss the frames the changes below produce. scene_expect at the
        # end judges what arrived.
        - action: scene_watch
        # The server compiled its skill catalog at startup; list it.
        - action: skill_catalog
        # Chat: say hello on the world channel (a bus call into the chat
        # service). The game fans the stored line back as a push (msg 10101)
        # to every online player, the sender included — but it pushes before
        # it answers, so the robot's own copy can land before wait_push is
        # listening and be dropped as an unsolicited push. Hence the selector:
        # a push within half a second (its own, or another robot's), or move
        # on; chat_history is the check that the line is there either way
        # (the reconnect path). The wait is short because it counts toward
        # the scenario cost the p95 threshold judges.
        - action: send_chat
          param:
            kind: world
            target: 1
            text: hello from a robot
        - selector:
            - action: wait_push
              param:
                msg: 10101
                timeout: 500ms
            - wait: 10ms
        - action: chat_history
          param:
            kind: world
            target: 1
            limit: 20
        - action: add_item
          param:
            item_id: 1001
            count: 1
        - action: add_exp
          param:
            amount: 150
        # The level-up sent a reward mail through the effect chain
        # (asynchronous, usually well under a second). Poll the mailbox until
        # it is there, then claim the attachment into the Bag: the mail
        # service's exactly-once claim around one Nest transaction.
        - retry:
            times: 20
            node:
              sequence:
                - wait: 250ms
                - action: list_mail
                  param:
                    limit: 20
        - action: claim_mail
        # Claim the same mail again. The mail service refuses a committed
        # mail, and the Player's ledger makes the grant a no-op if one ever
        # gets through — the bag must not grow either way.
        - action: claim_mail_replay
        # A gift to oneself: the saga debits the Bag (step 1), then mails
        # the item (step 2) — two transaction domains, one durable
        # operation. The saga starts asynchronously (start effect → outbox →
        # coordinator), so poll its status until terminal, expect it
        # completed, then the gift mail is in the box: list and claim it,
        # which puts the item back in the Bag.
        - action: send_gift_self
          param:
            item_id: 1001
            count: 1
        - retry:
            times: 40
            node:
              sequence:
                - wait: 250ms
                - action: gift_status
        - action: expect_gift
          param:
            status: completed
        - retry:
            times: 20
            node:
              sequence:
                - wait: 250ms
                - action: list_mail
                  param:
                    limit: 20
        - action: claim_mail
        # The compensation path: player 1 has never entered the game, so the
        # deliver step refuses, and the coordinator runs debit's
        # compensation — the item is back in the Bag, the saga ends
        # compensated. The second claim above left the Bag at one potion,
        # which this round-trip leaves untouched.
        - action: send_gift
          param:
            to_player_id: 1
            item_id: 1001
            count: 1
        - retry:
            times: 40
            node:
              sequence:
                - wait: 250ms
                - action: gift_status
        - action: expect_gift
          param:
            status: compensated
        # A dungeon run with the session service: open it (one live run per
        # player, idempotent per frame), then resolve it as a clear — which
        # grants exp through the same AddExp transaction and releases what
        # the run held.
        - action: enter_dungeon
          param:
            kind: dungeon
        - action: finish_dungeon
          param:
            success: true
        # Replay the same finish. The session service answers idempotently
        # (the run comes back as it stands), and the clear must not be paid
        # twice — the reward transaction records the run id, so a second
        # attempt grants nothing.
        - action: finish_dungeon_replay
          param:
            success: true
        # The dungeon clear moved this player's exp and level through an
        # ordinary Nest transaction. Nobody wrote a push for it: the entity's
        # sync subject produced a delta, the scene's room framed it, and the
        # client decoded it. If this fails, replication is not carrying real
        # state changes — the rest of the run would not notice.
        # Wear the sword. The item was granted by add_item above; equipping
        # takes it out of the bag and puts it on in ONE transaction, and the
        # attribute layer follows the worn set — so this is also what makes
        # the replicated attr_final move.
        - action: add_item
          param:
            item_id: 2001
            count: 1
        - action: equip
          param:
            item_id: 2001
        # The server-wide window: the clear above contributed one point to it,
        # and the replayed clear contributed none. Both halves are asserted in
        # the action, because "the replay was free" is only visible as an
        # exact count.
        - action: activity_standing
        # The guild: an entity that belongs to no process in particular.
        # Founding takes its distributed lock; reading "my guild" does not,
        # because the player keeps a copy of that one fact — and the two have
        # to agree, which is what the second step checks.
        # Founding takes the guild's distributed lock. It is idempotent per
        # (player, name), so the client may repeat it: a lost reply replays
        # the same answer instead of minting a second guild.
        #
        # There is no retry wrapper here on purpose. The one temporary answer
        # this endpoint gives — guild_busy (100020), while a restarted
        # process waits out its predecessor's lock lease — is not something a
        # tight client loop fixes: measured, the dispatch itself waits far
        # longer than the lease (W-2026-09-20-04). Wrapping it would hide that
        # behind a slow, green run.
        - action: found_guild
        - action: guild_info
        # A paid order, across the platform process: the endpoint signs a
        # callback, the platform service records and delivers it, the grant
        # lands in Redis and the game drains it into the bag. Then a second
        # order for the same product — a different order id, so it is paid
        # again and the bag moves again.
        - action: purchase
          param:
            product_id: potion_pack
        - action: purchase_again
          param:
            product_id: potion_pack
        # The map: one step, then a step off the edge of the world. The
        # second one must be refused — and must answer with the position the
        # player still has.
        - action: move
          param:
            x: 501
            y: 500
        - action: move_out_of_bounds
          param:
            x: 100000
            y: 100000
        - action: scene_expect
          param:
            # Everything below is asserted on THIS client's own subject, so
            # every action that produces one of these fields has to have run
            # already — hence this step sits after the clear, the equip and
            # the move rather than in the middle of them.
            #
            # Two subjects at least: this client's own state and one of the
            # monsters the scene spawned near the spawn point. Monsters go
            # through the same AOI and the same wire as players — if the
            # pipeline had a special case for "not a player", this is where it
            # would show.
            subjects: 2
            # attr_final is the composed attribute view: it is never stored
            # (the DAO field is nopersist) and only exists on the wire, so
            # asking for it here is what proves the attribute container is
            # actually wired into replication rather than just into Mongo.
            fields: level,exp,attr_base,attr_final,pos_x,equipment
            timeout: 10s
        # The leaderboard. The clear was counted through the rank service with
        # the run id as the request id, so the replay above did not count a
        # second time — the board is where that shows.
        - action: rank_top
          param:
            limit: 10
        # Matchmaking: join the duel queue, then poll until the game's
        # matchmaker has paired this robot with another one. The ticket id
        # comes from the blackboard, where join_queue's OnResp put it.
        - action: join_queue
          param:
            mode: duel
        # The matchmaker pushes MatchFound (msg 10100) to both players; wait
        # for it. If the push was missed (10s), fall back to polling the
        # ticket — the ticket carries the match id either way.
        - selector:
            - action: wait_push
              param:
                msg: 10100
                timeout: 10s
            - retry:
                times: 20
                node:
                  sequence:
                    - wait: 250ms
                    - action: poll_match
        # Read the ticket once more whichever way the match arrived: the
        # response carries the match id and the member order, and the battle
        # needs both (the push only wakes the robot up).
        - action: poll_match
        # The lockstep battle: this robot runs a real client of
        # roost-core/lockstep for the length of one match. Every BattleFrame
        # push (10102) is fed to the bot, the bot applies the frames to the
        # demo simulation in order and answers with this seat's input for the
        # next frame plus a hash every keyframe. Both seats must agree on
        # those hashes or the room rules a desync and the run fails.
        - action: battle
        # Ranked: the same dance on the level-scored queue. Every robot is
        # at the same level here, so the window is trivially satisfied; the
        # point is that the second queue and its ScoreWindow policy run.
        - action: join_queue
          param:
            mode: ranked
        - selector:
            - action: wait_push
              param:
                msg: 10100
                timeout: 10s
            - retry:
                times: 20
                node:
                  sequence:
                    - wait: 250ms
                    - action: poll_match
                      param:
                        mode: ranked
        - action: world_stats
